  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1212 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nA surface cold front currently stretches near the I-55 corridor\nin central IL, highlighted by and agitated cu field on visible \nsatellite imagery. Recent radar and satellite trends suggest \nthat convective initiation is already underway, a couple hours \nahead of what the high-res models have been suggesting. Ahead of\nthe front, surface dew points in the upper 60s to lower 70s \nsupport a moderately unstable environment, with RAP and HRRR \nsoundings indicating MLCAPE values reaching near 2500 J/kg by \nmid- afternoon. 0-6 km bulk shear profiles of 35 kt, augmented \nby a subtle 500 mb speed max translating eastward across central\nIllinois, create an environment favorable for supercells, \nparticularly over east central IL. The limiting factor in storm \ncoverage will be a lack of forcing for ascent, which will be \ntied to weak low level convergence and lift ahead of the mid \nlevel wave. Suite of CAMs generally show little or no \ndevelopment (HRRR, HRW, NAM Nest), with a couple notable \nexceptions (RRFS/FV3) showing more robust coverage over east\ncentral IL. Given the CAPE/shear profiles, sufficient lapse \nrates, and DCAPE up to 1400 J/kg, damaging wind gusts and large \nhail are the primary severe hazards. SPC maintains a 2% tornado \nrisk which would likely be tied to storm scale interactions. Any\nconvection should track southeast around 30 mph, and exit into \nIndiana by 9-10 pm.\n\nSurface high pressure builds southward from the Upper Midwest \nacross the Great Lakes region Thursday and Friday. This will\nbring a nice stretch of weather to the region with comfortable\ntemps and humidity for late Aug. Confidence remains good in dry\nconditions persisting through Saturday. Return southerly flow \naround the exiting surface high brings high temperatures into \nthe mid-80s with overnight lows in the low 60s.\n\nModel divergence increases regarding a shortwave trough \ntraversing the northern stream Sunday into early next week. \nDeterministic GFS runs feature a deeper mid-level trough driving\na cold front into Illinois. In contrast, deterministic ECMWF \nand ECMWF-AI runs maintain a flatter, displaced trough \nstructure, keeping mainly dry conditions going. Consequently, \nthe forecast relies heavily on NBM blend guidance, maintaining \nslight chance (20%) PoPs, primarily over the northern CWA.\n\nUpper ridging builds across the Midwest early next week,\nbringing a return to heat and humidity as surface flow becomes\nsoutherly. Highs are projected to reach the upper 80 to mid 90s,\nwith heat indices peaking in the upper 90s to around 100 Mon-Wed.",
